# Build AlchemyCLI AI — Local ML-Powered Developer Command Assistant

## 1. Project Overview

Build a production-quality open-source developer command assistant called:

# AlchemyCLI AI

Primary CLI:

```bash
alchemyai
```

The goal is to allow developers, DevOps engineers, SREs, cloud engineers, system administrators, and students to ask natural-language questions from the terminal and receive the most relevant, verified command or set of commands.

Examples:

```bash
alchemyai "how do I find pods using the most memory?"
```

```bash
alchemyai "how do I restart a kubernetes deployment?"
```

```bash
alchemyai "find which process is using port 8080"
```

```bash
alchemyai "how do I undo my last git commit but keep the changes?"
```

```bash
alchemyai "show kafka consumer group lag"
```

```bash
alchemyai "create a python virtual environment"
```

```bash
alchemyai "build a rust project in release mode"
```

```bash
alchemyai "find docker containers consuming the most memory"
```

The system must understand the intent of the question and retrieve the most appropriate command(s) from a verified command knowledge base.

---

# 2. Critical Design Principle

DO NOT build a model that freely generates shell commands.

The ML model should understand the user's intent and retrieve commands from a verified dataset.

Architecture:

```text
                       User Question
                            │
                            ▼
                    Python ML/NLP Layer
                            │
                    Intent + Embedding
                            │
                            ▼
                     Semantic Search
                            │
                            ▼
                Verified Command Knowledge Base
                            │
                            ▼
                    Ranked Candidates
                            │
                            ▼
                    Safety Validation
                            │
                            ▼
                       CLI Response
```

The ML model answers:

> "Which verified command(s) best match this question?"

It does NOT answer:

> "Invent a shell command."

This is a mandatory safety and correctness requirement.

